<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Li Bian</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">889–943</span> · <a href="/centuries/10th-century">10th century</a> · Also known as Xu Zhigao, Emperor Liezu of Southern Tang</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Li Bian, born Xu Zhigao, was the founder of the Southern Tang, one of the Ten Kingdoms of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>. Adopted son of the regent <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xu-wen">Xu Wen</a>, he took the throne of Wu in 937 and in 939 adopted the surname Li, claiming descent from the Tang imperial family. He ruled from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nanjing">Jinling</a> (Nanjing) until his death in 943, favouring peace and sound government.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Li Bian was born in 889 at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xuzhou">Pengcheng</a>, in modern Xuzhou, during the reign of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emperor-zhaozong-of-tang">Emperor Zhaozong of Tang</a>, and may have been called Li Pengnu as a child. His father, Li Rong, spent so much time with Buddhist monks that he was known as Philosopher Li. Li Rong died in 893 while the region was at war; his brother Li Qiu fled with the boy and his mother, Lady Liu, to Hao Prefecture, where she soon died, and Li Qiu left the child with the monks of the Kaiyuan Temple.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>In 908 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xu-wen">Xu Wen</a> and Zhang Hao assassinated Yang Wo, ruler of the Huainan state; Xu then killed Zhang and governed as regent for Yang Wo's brother Yang Longyan. Xu Zhigao, as Li Bian was then named, commanded the Yuancong Corps and from 909 served at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nanjing">Jinling</a>, then Sheng Prefecture, as deputy commander of the fleet. After a campaign of 912 against Li Yu, governor of Xuan Prefecture, he became prefect of Sheng, which he was said to govern well, choosing honest officials and gathering learned men such as the strategist Song Qiqiu.</p><p>After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xu-wen">Xu Wen</a>'s death his titles were divided. Xu Zhigao controlled the government, but Xu Zhixun held the largest army of the state at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nanjing">Jinling</a> and contested his decisions. In 931 Xu Zhigao moved his own headquarters to Jinling, leaving his son Xu Jingtong in charge at Guangling, and built the Lixian Pavilion, where he collected books and discussed affairs with scholars such as Sun Sheng.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>On taking the throne he honoured <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xu-wen">Xu Wen</a> as emperor and, instead of giving the deposed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yang-pu">Yang Pu</a> a noble title, presented him with an imperial title while still addressing him in the form of a subject. In spring 939, with his younger brothers among the petitioners, he agreed to return to the surname Li and claimed descent from Li Ke, a son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emperor-gaozong-of-tang">Emperor Gaozong of Tang</a>.</p><p>He refused an honorific imperial title as false beauty against ancient tradition. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/song-dynasty">Song dynasty</a> historian Sima Guang wrote that in declining such honours, and in keeping relatives and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eunuch">eunuchs</a> from improper power, no other state of the time could compare with Southern Tang. The kingdom did not reunite the Chinese realm, but it absorbed Min and Chu and became, with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chengdu">Chengdu</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hangzhou">Hangzhou</a>, one of the leading centres of learning.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Li Bian” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote><b>Xu Zhigao</b>, adopted son of the regent Xu Wen, took the throne of Wu (Ten Kingdoms) in 937.</blockquote><a href="/state/southern-tang">Southern Tang</a></li><li><blockquote>Yang Xingmi's sons were weak, and real power passed to the general Xu Wen and his adopted son <b>Xu Zhigao</b>, who ran the government from Jinling (Nanjing).</blockquote><a href="/state/wu-ten-kingdoms">Wu (Ten Kingdoms)</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nanjing">Nanjing</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/china">China</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=32.06,118.78&amp;year=924" aria-label="Nanjing on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="7606 997 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the place of death of Li Bian"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M8056 1267h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M8056 1267h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the place of death. <a href="/atlas?at=32.06,118.78&amp;year=924">What was here in 924 CE? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
